\subsection{Threshold Recommendation}

For the main diagnostic (Task~B2), we recommend $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ as the operating threshold for three reasons:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Maximum recall.} The $1.5\times$ threshold achieves 100\% recall, detecting all five CIVO cases including the transient case (009b, $\nve = 1.62\times$). Raising the threshold to $1.7\times$ loses 009b, dropping recall to 80.0\%.
\item \textbf{Best F1.} At $1.5\times$, F1 = 90.9\%, the highest among all tested thresholds for B2. The single false positive (008e\_v3\_s2) is mechanistically identifiable as IRD rather than CIVO.
\item \textbf{Sensitivity--specificity tradeoff.} Lower thresholds ($1.0\times$) add false positives without gaining true positives. Higher thresholds ($\geq 1.7\times$) sacrifice recall without eliminating the 008e\_v3\_s2 false positive (which requires $\tau > 3.78$).
\end{enumerate}

For Task~B1 (sustained CIVO only), the optimal threshold $\nve \geq 3.9\times$ achieves $10/11$ accuracy ($90.9\%$, 95\% CI: [58.7\%, 99.8\%]); the sole false positive is exp\_059 (NVE${=}4.72\times$, self-correcting CIVO classified as B1-negative).

\subsection{Expanded ROC Analysis}
\label{sec:roc_analysis}

The original $n{=}17$ analysis (Tables~\ref{tab:sensitivity_b1}--\ref{tab:sensitivity_b2_full}) predates the SGS, RAVE, and TCN experiments. We extend the NVE diagnostic evaluation to $n{=}30$ configurations spanning all tested interventions. Figure~\ref{fig:nve_roc} shows the receiver operating characteristic curve with 95\% bootstrap confidence interval (10{,}000 resamples).

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\columnwidth]{../../artifacts/figures/nve_roc_curve.pdf}
\caption{NVE ROC curve ($n{=}30$ configurations, AUROC${=}0.957$, in-sample, post-hoc). The preset $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ operating point (star) achieves TPR${=}1.00$, FPR${=}0.18$. Three false positives arise from non-CIVO value overestimation: RAVE anchor pollution, obs-decoder SGS side effects, and IRD eval-driven collapse (008e\_v3 seed~2). Threshold calibrated and validated in-sample, post-hoc; cross-architecture validation deferred.}
\label{fig:nve_roc}
\end{figure}

We label a configuration as CIVO-positive if and only if (i)~it uses a trained $\dt$-embedding with multi-rate data exposure, and (ii)~NVE exceeds $1.5\times$ with co-occurring entropy collapse. Configurations exhibiting high NVE through other mechanisms (gradient truncation side effects, value anchor pollution) are labeled non-CIVO.

The three false positives (FPR${=}0.18$) are mechanistically distinct from CIVO: (1)~RAVE anchor pollution (exp\_028, the EMA anchor becomes inflated, producing overestimation through a different pathway); (2)~obs-decoder SGS-induced value distortion (exp\_025 seed~2, blocking observation decoder gradients damages the RSSM latent space); (3)~IRD eval-driven collapse (exp\_008e\_v3 seed~2, actor entropy \emph{increases} with evaluation collapse, mechanistically distinct from CIVO's entropy \emph{decrease}). All three produce NVE $> 1.5\times$ through non-CIVO mechanisms, indicating that the NVE diagnostic detects value overestimation broadly rather than CIVO specifically---a feature rather than a limitation for practitioners monitoring deployment health. The closest sub-threshold case (008f seed~4, NVE${=}1.41\times$) lacks $\dt$-conditioning entirely ($d{=}0$); its elevated NVE reflects transient value noise that self-corrects without the positive feedback loop characteristic of CIVO. The closest above-threshold positive (008d seed~3, NVE${=}1.53\times$) exhibits the full CIVO causal chain: trained $\dt$-embedding, entropy decrease, and evaluation collapse to 14\% of baseline.

\paragraph{Comprehensive validation ($n{=}56$).}
Expanding the evaluation set to all classifiable experiments yields $n{=}56$ ($n_{\text{pos}}{=}24$, $n_{\text{neg}}{=}32$), spanning Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, and multiple intervention configurations (SGS, TCN, dual-critic, frozen embedding). At the $1.5\times$ threshold: AUROC${=}0.990$, precision${=}0.889$, recall${=}1.000$, F1${=}0.941$, accuracy${=}53/56$. The three false positives are: exp\_008e\_v3\_subsample ($1.93\times$, mitigation config with transient VE spike), exp\_047 ($1.71\times$, frozen embedding transient), and exp\_057 ($1.88\times$, frozen embedding transient). All 24 CIVO-positive configurations are correctly detected (FN${=}0$), with CIVO-positive NVE ranging from $1.53\times$ (008d seed~3, late-onset) to $70.64\times$ (010b seed~0, Cheetah). The substantial increase in sample size from $n{=}32$ to $n{=}56$ strengthens confidence in the $1.5\times$ threshold as a robust diagnostic boundary.

\subsection{NVE Normalization Robustness}
\label{sec:nve_robustness}

To assess sensitivity to baseline seed choice, we extend the single-seed baseline (seed~1, eval 915.7 at 105K) to three additional seeds trained to 505K steps (Table~\ref{tab:baseline_seeds}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Walker Walk baseline seeds at 105K and 505K steps.}
\label{tab:baseline_seeds}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Seed} & \textbf{Eval@105K} & \textbf{Eval@505K} & \textbf{ve@65K} & \textbf{ve@105K} \\
\midrule
s1 & 915.7 & 970.4 & 0.563 & 0.656 \\
s2 & 443.5 & 945.7 & 0.898 & 0.742 \\
s3 & 869.0 & 947.7 & --- & 0.664 \\
s4 & 922.8 & 938.9 & --- & 0.624 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Seed~2 exhibits slow convergence (eval 443.5 at 105K, recovering to 945.7 by 512K), confirming non-pathological slow convergence rather than seed failure. Its elevated $v/e$ ratio at 65K (0.898 vs.\ 0.563 for seed~1) reflects the low evaluation denominator rather than pathological value overestimation. Using seed~2 as the sole normalization reference systematically deflates NVE values, causing two marginal CIVO-positive configurations (008d seed~3, peak NVE $1.53\times$; 009b seed~2, peak NVE $1.62\times$) to fall below the $1.5\times$ threshold. Core CIVO cases (peak NVE $> 3\times$) remain correctly classified under all baseline seeds.

Single-seed normalization (s1) yields AUROC $= 0.933$ and accuracy 7/8 (87.5\%) on the Walker subset. Four-seed mean normalization yields AUROC $= 0.867$ and accuracy 6/8 (75.0\%): the boundary case exp\_009b flips from $\nve = 1.62\times$ (s1) to $1.23\times$ (4-seed mean), because its peak occurs at 45K when the s1 $v/e$ ratio (0.331) is well below the 4-seed mean (0.436). All high-NVE configurations remain correctly classified (006: $4.84\times \to 4.52\times$; 008d: $4.12\times \to 3.54\times$). The $1.5\times$ threshold thus correctly identifies all strong CIVO cases regardless of baseline choice; sensitivity is confined to configurations within 10\% of the threshold.

\paragraph{Normalization conventions.} Walker NVE is normalized against the single-seed baseline (seed~1, exp\_001, 505K steps). Cheetah NVE is normalized against a 5-seed mean baseline (seeds~0--4, each run to ${\sim}$210K steps). The differing seed counts reflect available baseline data; both provide sufficient coverage of the respective experimental windows (Walker: 210K analysis window; Cheetah: 105K analysis window).

\paragraph{Limitations.} The initial $n{=}11$ analysis had wide Clopper-Pearson confidence intervals (e.g., 90.9\% accuracy, 95\% CI [58.7\%, 99.8\%]). Progressive expansion to $n{=}30$ (in-sample, AUROC${=}0.957$; Figure~\ref{fig:nve_roc}) and ultimately $n{=}56$ (comprehensive validation, AUROC${=}0.990$, F1${=}0.941$) substantially tightens these bounds. Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity_b2_full} reports the $n{=}17$ two-environment core evaluation. Cross-architecture validation is deferred to future work.

