The replication confirms the original fidelity gap pattern with two additional observations. First, the collapsed model achieves the \emph{lowest} absolute obs\_MSE at $\dt{=}1$ (9.96 vs.\ baseline 16.08), indicating that the trained $\dt$-embedding improves world model quality overall---the pathology is selective degradation of reward predictions at longer horizons, not general model failure. Second, freezing the $\dt$-embedding reduces the reward gap from $3.11\times$ (training-time; $2.7\times$ in independent replication) to $1.96\times$, providing converging evidence for its role in preventing the co-adaptation loop while leaving residual multi-step reward prediction difficulty.

